I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.records.recover_json_object. Its documented rule says: “The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text, because a model places its final answer last and marks it: a reply that quotes its previous answer and then gives a corrected one is scored on the correction.” The two reply texts are as follows. First: '```\nHere is an object: {"a": 1}\n```\n{"b": 2}'. Second: '{"b": 2}'. For the first text, the rule requires the answer {"a": 1}, because a code fence holds an object and the last object inside a code fence is {"a": 1}. For the second text, the rule requires {"b": 2}, because no code fence holds an object, so the last top-level object is {"b": 2}. The check recover_json_object gives the same answer for both: it returns {"b": 2} for each, because its fenced pool only contains the entire fenced content 'Here is an object: {"a": 1}', which does not parse as a dict, so it falls through to the top-level pool and takes the last top-level object. I would be wrong if recover_json_object returns {"a": 1} for the first text and {"b": 2} for the second, or if the rule is read to mean that a fence holds an object only when the entire fenced content is itself a JSON object.